 ],
 "literal": "If you ask, 'Who is your king?' Our king / filters matured strong toddy for field workers, / satisfying their desires with boiled turtle meat, / stuffing their mouths with rich roasted spiny eel fish, / in whose prosperous good country daily work ceases but unceasing festivals continue, / who is the enemy of hunger for the distressed relatives of bards, / is the lord of Kozhi, Kopperuncozhan, / who, befriended in flawless friendship with Potti, / gives us a mouth-filling great smile every single day.",
 "translation": "If you ask, 'Who is your king?' Our king is he who filters matured strong toddy for his field workers, satisfying their desires with boiled turtle meat, stuffing their mouths with rich roasted spiny eel fish. Even in his prosperous good country, where daily labor ceases but unceasing festivals go on, he stands as the enemy of hunger for the distressed families of bards. He is Kopperuncozhan, the lord of Kozhi, who, closely befriended in flawless friendship with Potti, bestows upon us a mouth-filling great smile every single day.",
 "modern": "உங்களுடைய அரசன் யார் என்று கேட்டால், எங்களுடைய அரசன்: வயல் உழவர்களுக்கு வடித்த முதிர்ந்த கள்ளையும், அவர்களுடைய ஆசை தீர ஆமைப் புழுக்கலையும், ஆரல் மீனின் கொழுத்த சுட்ட கறியையும் வாயில் அடையும்படி தந்து, அன்றாட வேலைகள் ஓய்ந்தாலும் ஓயாத விழாக்கள் கொண்டாடும் வளமான நல்ல நாட்டிலும், பாணர்களின் வறிய சுற்றத்தாரின் பசிக்கு பகையாகி அதைப் போக்கி, உறையூரை ஆளும் கோப்பெருஞ்சோழன் ஆவான். அவன், குற்றமற்ற நட்பினைக் கொண்ட பொத்தியாரோடு கூடி, நமக்கு நாள்தோறும் வாய் நிறைந்த பெரிய மகிழ்ச்சியையும் புன்னகையையும் தருபவன் ஆவான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the pāṭāṇ tiṇai (praising a king's virtues) and iyaṉmoḻi tuṟai (describing the natural qualities of a king). The poet Picirāntaiyār, though living far away in the Pandya country, had a deep spiritual friendship with Kopperuncozhan. Here, he proudly describes his king's immense generosity to both common laborers and visiting bards. The vivid imagery of field workers enjoying rich food and toddy, and the country celebrating endless festivals, highlights the prosperity and joy under the king's rule. The poet also beautifully mentions the king's flawless friendship with the poet Potti, showing that the king values intellectual and emotional bonds as much as royal duties.",
